<main>
  <header>
    <h1>China Visa for the Canton Fair 2026: Do You Need One?</h1>
    <p>Last verified: September 16, 2026</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <h2>Quick Answer</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Whether you need a China visa for the 2026 Canton Fair depends on your passport, itinerary, and the entry policy you plan to use.</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      Some travelers may qualify for visa-free entry or China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy, while others will need an appropriate Chinese visa before traveling.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you plan to use the 240-hour visa-free transit policy, your trip must meet the policy's transit requirements. A simple round trip such as U.S. → Guangzhou → U.S. does not satisfy the third-country-or-region transit rule.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Canton Fair 2026 Visa Options at a Glance</h2>

    <p>
      There is no single visa rule that applies to every Canton Fair visitor. The right entry option depends on factors such as your nationality, itinerary, length of stay, and purpose of travel.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Entry option</th>
            <th>Who it may suit</th>
            <th>Key point</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Visa-free entry (non-transit)</strong></td>
            <td>Travelers whose nationality and trip qualify under a current Chinese visa-exemption policy</td>
            <td>No third-country transit is necessarily required, but the specific nationality, purpose, and stay rules of that policy must be met.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>240-hour visa-free transit</strong></td>
            <td>Eligible travelers genuinely transiting Mainland China on the way to a different country or region</td>
            <td>Your itinerary must meet the transit rule, and you must also satisfy the passport, onward-ticket, port, permitted-area, and stay requirements.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Chinese visa</strong></td>
            <td>Travelers who do not qualify for an applicable visa-free policy or whose itinerary does not meet the transit requirements</td>
            <td>An M visa is intended for commercial and trade activities. The exact visa and supporting documents depend on your circumstances and where you apply.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      For many international buyers, the first question is therefore not simply "Which visa should I apply for?" but <strong>"Do I need a visa for my particular Canton Fair trip at all?"</strong>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Can You Attend the Canton Fair Using China's 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit?</h2>

    <p>
      Potentially, yes. China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy allows eligible travelers to enter through designated ports, remain within permitted areas for up to 240 hours, and engage in short-term activities including business.
    </p>

    <p>
      That means attending the Canton Fair as a business visitor may be compatible with the policy, provided you meet all of its requirements.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, eligibility is not based only on how long you plan to stay. Your nationality, passport, itinerary, entry and exit points, onward ticket, permitted travel area, and other conditions must also qualify.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/china-240-hour-visa-free-transit/">
        See our complete China 240-Hour Visa-Free Transit Guide for the full eligibility rules.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Most Important Rule: Your Trip Must Be a Genuine Transit</h2>

    <p>
      To use the 240-hour visa-free transit policy, you must be traveling through Mainland China on the way to a <strong>different country or region</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Think of the route as:
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Country or Region A → Mainland China → Country or Region B</strong>
    </p>

    <p>
      A and B refer to the country or region immediately before and immediately after Mainland China in your itinerary. They must be different for the transit-route requirement to be satisfied.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Example itinerary</th>
            <th>Transit-route test</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>United States → Guangzhou → United States</td>
            <td><strong>Does not qualify</strong></td>
            <td>You return to the same country you came from, so this is not transit to a third country or region.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>United States → Guangzhou → Japan</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>You enter Mainland China from the United States and continue to a different country.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>United States → Guangzhou → South Korea</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>South Korea is different from the country you entered China from.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>United States → Guangzhou → Hong Kong</td>
            <td><strong>May qualify</strong></td>
            <td>Hong Kong is treated separately from Mainland China for the transit-route test.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      Passing the route test does not by itself guarantee eligibility. You must still meet all other requirements of the 240-hour visa-free transit policy.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Can U.S. Citizens Attend the Canton Fair Without a China Visa?</h2>

    <p>
      Potentially, yes. U.S. citizens are currently among the nationalities eligible for China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy.
    </p>

    <p>
      But U.S. citizenship alone does not make a Canton Fair trip visa-free. Your itinerary and all other eligibility requirements must also qualify. In particular, the route must meet the third-country-or-region transit rule explained above.
    </p>

    <p>
      If your trip does not qualify for 240-hour visa-free transit or another applicable visa-free policy, you should determine which Chinese visa is appropriate before traveling.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/do-us-citizens-need-a-visa-for-china/">
        Read our full guide to China visa requirements for U.S. citizens.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>When Do You Need a Regular China Visa Instead?</h2>

    <p>
      If you do not qualify for an applicable visa-free policy, you will generally need to obtain the appropriate Chinese visa before traveling to the Canton Fair.
    </p>

    <p>
      This may apply, for example, if:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>your nationality is not eligible for the visa-free policy you hoped to use;</li>
      <li>your itinerary does not satisfy the 240-hour transit requirement;</li>
      <li>your planned stay is longer than the visa-free policy allows;</li>
      <li>your entry or exit arrangements do not meet the applicable port or geographic requirements; or</li>
      <li>your planned activities fall outside the scope of the visa-free policy.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>What about the M visa?</h3>

    <p>
      China's M visa is intended for people entering China for commercial and trade activities. For a traveler attending the Canton Fair for business purposes, this may be the relevant conventional visa category if a visa is required.
    </p>

    <p>
      Visa application requirements can vary depending on your nationality and the Chinese embassy, consulate, or visa center where you apply. Supporting documents for an M visa may include documents related to the commercial activity or an invitation from a trade fair or business partner.
    </p>

    <p>
      Do not assume that another traveler's document list will apply to you. Check the current requirements for the Chinese visa office responsible for your application before submitting it.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Do You Need a Canton Fair Invitation Letter for Your Visa?</h2>

    <p>
      A Canton Fair invitation letter and a Chinese visa are not the same thing.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you need to apply for a Chinese visa, an invitation letter may be one of the supporting documents relevant to your application, depending on the visa category and the requirements of the Chinese embassy, consulate, or visa center handling your case.
    </p>

    <p>
      For commercial and trade travel, current M-visa guidance may accept documentation such as an invitation issued by a trade fair in China or other documents related to the commercial activity.
    </p>

    <p>
      However, having a Canton Fair invitation does not by itself give you permission to enter Mainland China. You must still qualify for the visa or visa-free policy you plan to use.
    </p>

    <p>
      Likewise, travelers who qualify for visa-free entry or 240-hour visa-free transit should not assume that an invitation letter is automatically required for immigration purposes simply because they are attending the Canton Fair.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="https://www.cantonfair.org.cn/en-US/pages/790884271194980352" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
        See the Canton Fair's official invitation application guide.
      </a>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>China Visa vs. Canton Fair Invitation vs. Buyer Registration</h2>

    <p>
      These three items serve different purposes and are easy to confuse.
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Item</th>
            <th>What it is for</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>China visa or applicable visa-free policy</strong></td>
            <td>Determines whether you are permitted to enter Mainland China.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Canton Fair invitation letter</strong></td>
            <td>May support a Chinese visa application and can also be part of the Canton Fair's visitor process, depending on your circumstances.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Canton Fair buyer registration / buyer badge</strong></td>
            <td>
              Relates to your registration and access to the Canton Fair itself. It is separate from Chinese immigration permission.
              <a href="https://buyer.cantonfair.org.cn/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                Use the official Buyer Service System.
              </a>
            </td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      In other words, being registered for the Canton Fair does not replace the need to meet China's entry requirements, and having permission to enter China does not automatically complete your Canton Fair registration.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>140th Canton Fair 2026 Dates</h2>

    <p>
      The 140th Canton Fair will take place in Guangzhou in three phases:
    </p>

    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Phase</th>
            <th>Dates</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Phase 1</strong></td>
            <td>October 15–19, 2026</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Phase 2</strong></td>
            <td>October 23–27, 2026</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Phase 3</strong></td>
            <td>October 31–November 4, 2026</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>
      The phase you attend can matter when planning your arrival, departure, and total time in China. If you intend to rely on the 240-hour visa-free transit policy, make sure your entire itinerary fits within the policy's permitted stay and routing rules.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Canton Fair 2026 Entry Checklist</h2>

    <p>
      Before traveling, check each of the following separately:
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Passport:</strong> confirm that your passport meets the requirements of the visa or visa-free policy you plan to use.</li>
      <li><strong>China entry permission:</strong> determine whether you need a Chinese visa or qualify for an applicable visa-free policy.</li>
      <li><strong>Transit itinerary:</strong> if using 240-hour visa-free transit, confirm that you are traveling onward to a different country or region and that your route meets the policy rules.</li>
      <li><strong>Onward ticket:</strong> have a confirmed ticket showing your departure from China within the permitted period.</li>
      <li><strong>Entry and exit ports:</strong> confirm that the ports you plan to use are permitted under the policy.</li>
      <li><strong>Permitted travel area:</strong> make sure your planned travel within China stays within the area allowed under the visa-free transit policy.</li>
      <li><strong>Canton Fair registration:</strong> complete the buyer registration or badge process required for entry to the fair.</li>
      <li><strong>Invitation documents:</strong> if you are applying for a visa and an invitation is required for your application, prepare the appropriate supporting documents.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      Entry policies can change, so verify the current official rules again before booking or traveling. Meeting the published requirements also does not guarantee admission; Chinese immigration authorities make the final entry decision.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>Do I need a China visa to attend the Canton Fair in 2026?</h3>

    <p>
      Not necessarily. Whether you need a visa depends on your nationality, itinerary, length of stay, and the entry policy you qualify for.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some travelers may qualify for a visa-free entry policy or for China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy. If no applicable visa-free option covers your trip, you will generally need an appropriate Chinese visa before traveling.
    </p>

    <h3>Can I use 240-hour visa-free transit to attend the Canton Fair?</h3>

    <p>
      Potentially, yes. Eligible travelers using the 240-hour visa-free transit policy may engage in short-term business activities in China.
    </p>

    <p>
      You must still satisfy all of the policy requirements, including nationality, passport, onward-ticket, port, permitted-area, stay-length, and genuine-transit requirements.
    </p>

    <h3>Can I fly from the United States to Guangzhou and then return directly to the United States?</h3>

    <p>
      Not under the 240-hour visa-free transit route rule. A route such as <strong>United States → Guangzhou → United States</strong> does not involve onward travel to a different country or region.
    </p>

    <p>
      A route such as <strong>United States → Guangzhou → Japan</strong> may satisfy the transit-route requirement, although all other policy conditions must also be met.
    </p>

    <h3>Can U.S. citizens attend the Canton Fair without a Chinese visa?</h3>

    <p>
      Potentially. U.S. citizens are currently among the nationalities eligible for China's 240-hour visa-free transit policy, but the itinerary and all other requirements must also qualify.
    </p>

    <p>
      <a href="/do-us-citizens-need-a-visa-for-china/">
        See our full guide to China visa requirements for U.S. citizens.
      </a>
    </p>

    <h3>Do I need a Canton Fair invitation letter?</h3>

    <p>
      It depends on your circumstances. An invitation may be relevant as supporting documentation if you are applying for a Chinese visa, and the Canton Fair also has its own invitation process for overseas buyers.
    </p>

    <p>
      An invitation letter is not itself a Chinese visa and does not replace the need to meet China's immigration requirements.
    </p>

    <h3>Do I always need an M visa to attend the Canton Fair?</h3>

    <p>
      No. A traveler who qualifies for an applicable visa-free policy may not need to obtain a conventional visa for that trip.
    </p>

    <p>
      If you do need a visa for a business trip to the Canton Fair, the M visa is the Chinese visa category intended for commercial and trade activities. Check the requirements that apply to your particular application before traveling.
    </p>

    <h3>Can one 240-hour visa-free stay cover all three phases of the 140th Canton Fair?</h3>

    <p>
      No single 240-hour stay is long enough to remain in China continuously from the beginning of Phase 1 through the end of Phase 3.
    </p>

    <p>
      Do not assume that leaving and re-entering automatically solves this issue. Any separate entry would need to satisfy the applicable immigration rules independently, and Chinese immigration authorities make the final admission decision.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Before You Travel</h2>

    <p>
      China's visa and visa-free entry policies can change. Before relying on any exemption, verify the current official rules for your nationality and exact itinerary.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Last verified: September 16, 2026</strong>
    </p>
